A stiff copper wire is balanced horizontally on a pivot, as shown in Fig. 8.1.

{{figure:figure_8_1.png}}

Sections PQ, QR and RS of the wire are situated in a uniform magnetic field of flux density $B$ produced between the poles of a permanent magnet.
The perpendicular distance of PQRS from the pivot is $7.5\text{ cm}$.

When a current of $2.7\text{ A}$ is passed through the wire, a small mass of $45\text{ mg}$ is placed a distance $8.8\text{ cm}$ from the pivot in order to restore the balance of the wire, as shown in Fig. 8.2.

{{figure:figure_8_2.png}}
